CLASSES (hold behavior)

RateLimiter
	Its job is to run the whole sequence of responding to a request: get the membership, call the store, build the Decision, apply the fallback policy if the store fails.
	Doesnt need to know about the org service (it only talks to MappingCache), or the store's lock and key layout.
	Builds the ScopeLimit list (user first, then team, then company) and wraps only the try_admit call in try/except StoreUnavailable. Any other exception is a bug and is not caught.

LogStore
	It owns the log of which requests were accepted per key. Will handle request retries (seen request_id) and enforcing TTL on stale logs. The store owns the atomic check and write in one method, try_admit(scopes, request_id). It reads the clock itself, once per call. This keeps the lock near the data it protects, and a Redis-backed store can implement the same method later.
	Convention: scopes[0] is the user level. The duplicate check runs on that key only, because an accepted request is written to every level.
	It gets a list of ScopeLimit (scope name, id, limit), not Limits. The store builds the key string itself (e.g. "user:42"), so the key layout lives next to the storage. In a Redis version that is also where the shard hash tag goes.
	It treats the scope name as an opaque label. It doesnt need to know about the org mapping, the cache, Decision, channel, or what "user", "team" and "company" mean.
	Gets the clock.

MappingCache
	Handles storage of known user,team,company relationships for a fixed period of freshness. Returns those mappings if the keys are present and fresh, else asks OrgLookup once. Entries expire on a TTL, nobody tells the cache they are stale. An unknown user (None) is not cached, so a new user is found on the next call.
	Gets the clock for its TTL and an OrgLookup to call.
	Doesnt need to know about requests, responses, limits or counting.

FailOpen, FailClosed (implementations of FallbackPolicy, see below)


INTERFACES (what we depend on, someone else can fill in)

OrgLookup
	Our side of the org service. lookup(user_id) -> Membership | None.
	We do not design the real service. In tests a dict plays it.

FallbackPolicy (Strategy)
	One method: on_store_failure() -> Decision. Two implementations:
	FailOpen -> Decision(ALLOWED, fallback=True). Used for EMAIL.
	FailClosed -> Decision(REJECTED, rejection_reason=STORE_UNAVAILABLE, fallback=True). Used for SMS.
	The policy does not take the channel. RateLimiter receives a dict[Channel, FallbackPolicy] from config, picks by channel and calls it. So a policy is reusable for any channel, and changing a channel's behavior is a config change.
	Does not know why the store failed, or anything about users, teams or counting.


DATACLASSES (frozen, data only, no logic)

Decision
	composes the final decision information to be passed to the requesting service.
	Fields: outcome, rejection_reason, retry_after, fallback, error_reason.
	does not need to be involved in any process, just its member vars.

AdmitResult
	The store's answer to try_admit. Not the Decision, which is the caller-facing type.
	Fields: status (AdmitStatus), blocked_scopes (the scope names of the full levels, empty unless REJECTED), retry_after (seconds until the latest of the full levels frees a slot, only meaningful when REJECTED).
	The store computes retry_after because it holds the timestamps and the clock: the longest wait among the full levels, from each level's oldest entry. The limiter does not turn blocked_scopes into a reason, because the Decision has one RATE_LIMITED for every level. blocked_scopes is for logs and metrics.

ScopeLimit
	One level to check: scope (opaque name such as "user"), id, limit.
	The limiter builds one per level from Membership and Limits. The store turns scope and id into a key.

Limits
	pulled from config, passed to RateLimiter at construction.
	Fields: user_cap, team_cap, company_cap, window_seconds.
	Numbers only. Does not know how they are enforced or where the data lives.

Membership
	(team_id, company_id). Result given by MappingCache.


ENUMS (fixed value sets)

AdmitStatus: ACCEPTED, SEEN, REJECTED
Channel: EMAIL, SMS. Push is out of scope for now.
Outcome: ALLOWED, REJECTED, ERROR
RejectionReason: RATE_LIMITED, STORE_UNAVAILABLE
ErrorReason: UNKNOWN_USER


EXCEPTIONS

StoreUnavailable
	Raised by a LogStore when it cannot answer (timeout, lost connection). A real store catches its client library's errors and re-raises this, so RateLimiter depends on our exception and not on Redis. The in-memory LogStore never raises it.


CALLABLES (passed in, not classes)

clock: Callable[[], float]
	Received by MappingCache and LogStore.
